Hello,

Having done a pilot study last year, I prepared some methylation libraries using EM-Seq and decided to analyse my data using methylkit. I noticed no issues while doing the pilot study, as my sequencing data was quite small (around 6 million single-end reads per sample). However, for the full study, I got around 136 million paired-end reads per sample).

I have concerns regarding the number of CpGs I obtained using methylKit. It is known that there are 28 million CpGs in the human genome; however, the number of CpGs I got, especially at 1X and 3X coverage, suggests that the CpG counts may have been overestimated.

Please look at the snippets of my codes below and the results I got for each instance.

N/B: I did the alignment and methylation extraction with Bismark, then imported the Coverage (.cov) or alignment output (.bam) files into methylkit.

#Import and define the list containing the Bismark Coverage files
OG8861 <- list(
  "AAFJNNMM5-8861-04-00-01_S1_L001_R1_001_val_1_bismark_bt2_pe.bismark.cov.gz",
  "AAFJNNMM5-8861-05-00-01_S2_L001_R1_001_val_1_bismark_bt2_pe.bismark.cov.gz",
  "AAFJNNMM5-8861-06-00-01_S3_L001_R1_001_val_1_bismark_bt2_pe.bismark.cov.gz")

COV_1 <- methRead(OG8861,
                  sample.id <http://sample.id> = list("S1", "S2", "S3"),
                  pipeline = "bismarkCoverage",
                  assembly = "hg19",
                  treatment = c(0,0,1),
                  context = "CpG",
                  mincov = 1,
                  dbtype = "tabix”)

I repeated this for 3X, 5X, and 10X coverage (codes not shown)

Result:

