Hello Mark,

Thanks for your detailed question. Your code and approach seem correct. Also, it is true that there are 28 million CpGs in the human genome. However, the number of sites observed is approximately double the real number because many sites cover the same CpG-dinucleotide from both strands. Most sites with only 1bp distance very likely belong to the same CpG-dinucleotide. The problem is that the Bismark coverage files do not contain any strand information, so this could be misleading downstream. But using the Bismark BAM  or cytosine report files will retain strand information and allow "destranding" during the unite step, i.e. merging of sites that belong the same CpG-dinucleotide.

I hope this explanation helps to resolve your problem.

Best,
Alex

